Mild localized envenomation from insect sting (bee/wasp) to body part/area displaying bite reaction
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Immediate sharp localized pain, Erythematous wheal with central punctum, Complete absence of systemic allergic signs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-011
Reason for Visit: Acute rapidly swelling and intensely painful localized skin lesion following an insect sting, requiring urgent assessment for anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage confirmed sudden onset of pain and tissue swelling post-envenomation. Urgent assessment was required to definitively exclude evolving anaphylaxis, airway compromise, or secondary cellulitis before daytime medical services became available.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was stung by something a few hours ago, and the pain is burning terribly and the swelling is getting tighter.
History of Present Illness: The event began with immediate sharp, burning pain at the precise site of the sting. Within minutes, there was rapid development of a small, localized erythematous wheal. Over the subsequent two hours, localized pruritus emerged as the initial sharp pain subsided. Patient reports mild, non-progressive localized edema restricted to the immediate few centimeters surrounding the sting. There is a complete absence of systemic signs: no wheezing, stridor, generalized urticaria, or angioedema of the lips, tongue, or airway. Peak intense pain has resolved, leaving residual mild tenderness.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient exhibits high anxiety regarding severe allergic reactions.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, mildly anxious but not in acute respiratory distress. Speaking in full sentences.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment reveals an erythematous wheal with a central punctum on the affected limb. Palpation elicits immediate sharp localized pain. The surrounding skin is warm to the touch, mimicking early cellulitis, but the edema is well-circumscribed. There is a complete absence of systemic allergic signs, wheal spread, or generalized urticaria. No regional lymphadenopathy or ascending lymphangitis streaks are observed. Respiratory exam reveals clear, equal air entry bilaterally without stridor or wheeze.
Environmental Observations: Patient outdoors gardening at time of injury; no active infestation identified.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Took oral antihistamine prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild localized envenomation from insect sting - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out due to: Normal work of breathing, absence of hypotension, airway swelling, or generalized total-body rash.

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Onset over minutes rather than days, and lack of ascending lymphangitic spread or fever.

Retained foreign body (stinger) granuloma - Ruled out due to: Visual absence of retained barb within the central punctum.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation following envenomation triggered a high-acuity response to evaluate for systemic anaphylactic shock. Direct clinical observation and physical examination definitively isolated the pathology to a local histamine reaction. The urgent after-hours assessment was medically necessary to provide life-preserving clearance of airway compromise, administer targeted topical therapy, and de-escalate severe patient anxiety, which could not be safely deferred until the next standard in-hours consultation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered topical corticosteroid cream and recommended a short course of oral antihistamines to limit localized histamine release.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a covered ice pack directly to the sting site for 15 minutes every hour. This causes local vasoconstriction, immediately reducing both the painful burning sensation and the localized swelling.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ice packs.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours only if the redness begins to expand significantly.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if you develop sudden difficulty breathing, swelling of the lips or tongue, a hoarse voice, or a widespread itchy rash over your entire body.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Localized reactions can sometimes expand over 24-48 hours before receding; this is a normal localized response, not an infection.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain resolves quickly. The redness and swelling will peak at 24 hours and slowly fade over 3 to 5 days, transitioning into a persistent itch.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care immediately if you feel dizzy, if your throat feels tight, or if you begin to wheeze.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Antihistamines block the chemicals released by the sting, directly stopping the swelling and severe itch at a cellular level.
Actionable Steps: Do not scratch the sting site, as this introduces bacteria. Apply ice for rapid pain relief. Take the oral antihistamine before bed to ensure unbroken sleep.

SMS INFORMATION

Mild insect sting reaction diagnosed. No severe allergy. Use ice packs and oral antihistamines. Do not scratch.

SMS EDUCATION

Apply an ice pack wrapped in a cloth to the sting for 15 minutes to reduce swelling. Take your antihistamine pill tonight.

Minor chemical irritation from household cleaning product splash to conjunctiva
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Immediate ocular stinging post-exposure, Diffuse conjunctival injection, Normal limbal vascularity and return to neutral pH

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-012
Reason for Visit: Acute severe eye pain and tearing following chemical splash, requiring urgent sight-saving evaluation and irrigation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute chemical ocular exposure with immediate pain and vision changes. Urgent bedside assessment and intervention were absolutely critical to initiate immediate therapeutic irrigation, assess corneal structural integrity, and prevent irreversible alkaline or acidic tissue necrosis before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: A splash of bathroom cleaner got directly into my eye, and it is burning intensely and won't stop watering.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports immediate onset of unilateral ocular stinging and burning pain following a droplet splash from a household surface cleaner. This was followed by profuse reflex lacrimation and involuntary blepharospasm making it difficult to keep the eye open. Patient notes subjective photophobia and heightened sensitivity to bright room light. Visual acuity remains near baseline without profound blurring once the tears are cleared.
Functional Status: Temporarily visually impaired due to severe reflex tearing and photophobia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Cannot safely drive or mobilize through the house due to visual distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, holding a damp cloth over the affected eye, displaying significant discomfort in ambient lighting.
Physical Exam: Ocular examination demonstrates immediate ocular stinging post-exposure and profuse reflex tearing. There is diffuse bulbar and palpebral conjunctival injection. Inspection reveals normal limbal vascularity without blanching. Fluorescein staining reveals diffuse superficial punctate epithelial erosions; there are no large, deep corneal defects. Pupillary size and reactivity to light are normal. Copious volumetric irrigation with 1L of sterile saline resulted in a return to neutral pH (7.0) on standard litmus testing and substantial relief of intense symptoms.
Environmental Observations: Cleaning supplies left open on the counter; patient advised to secure them.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine ocular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor chemical irritation of conjunctiva - Status: Improving post-irrigation
Differentials:

Severe alkaline burn - Ruled out due to: Absence of limbal ischemia (blanching) and rapid return to neutral pH with 1L irrigation.

Corneal ulceration - Ruled out due to: Fluorescein demonstrating only diffuse punctate erosions rather than a large, focal coalescent defect.

Acute angle-closure glaucoma - Ruled out due to: Clear traumatic chemical mechanism, normal reactive pupil, and absence of a hard, stony globe.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Chemical exposures to the eye represent true ophthalmic emergencies until proven otherwise. Immediate on-site intervention was mandated to perform high-volume ocular irrigation, perform fluorescein assessment, and conduct pH testing. The urgent after-hours visit was critical to definitively clear the patient of severe, vision-threatening corrosive injury and stabilize the superficial epithelial damage, bypassing delayed presentation to an emergency department and ensuring tissue preservation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed 1 Liter sterile saline ocular irrigation. Prescribed topical preservative-free lubricating eye drops. Advised against the use of topical anesthetics for home use due to corneal toxicity risk.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instill the prescribed lubricating drops every 2 hours while awake. The aggressive lubrication physically coats the micro-abrasions, preventing eyelid friction during blinking and providing immediate, continuous pain relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or Optometrist review in 24 hours to ensure epithelial healing is progressing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if your vision suddenly drops, if a white hazy patch appears over the colored part of your eye, or if the severe burning pain returns.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Corneal healing is dynamic; secondary infections can develop if the eye is rubbed or poorly lubricated.
Clinical Course: The foreign body sensation and light sensitivity will be worst for the first 24 hours, followed by rapid improvement as the superficial cells regenerate over 2-3 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the pain becomes unbearable again, if you completely lose vision in that eye, or if thick yellow pus develops.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The eye drops act as an artificial protective shield, allowing the damaged surface cells underneath to rapidly heal without being repeatedly scraped by your eyelid.
Actionable Steps: Wear sunglasses indoors to reduce pain from light. Do not rub the eye under any circumstances. Use the lubricating drops frequently.

SMS INFORMATION

Minor chemical eye splash. Eye washed to neutral pH. Use prescribed lubricating drops every 2 hours. Mandatory GP/Optometrist review tomorrow.

SMS EDUCATION

